				<string name="Name">RaceServer</string>
				<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
				<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
				<UniqueId name="UniqueId">00539dfaaa2021f70ad4d4470000186c</UniqueId>
			</Properties>
			<Item class="ModuleScript" referent="RBX8094DCE758844089B73B0F853F51E4E4">
				<Properties>
					<Content name="LinkedSource"><null></null></Content>
					<ProtectedString name="Source"><![CDATA[--[[
	TRACKS  —  every track players can vote for. RaceServer builds them.

	Each one should feel like a level in a video game: a string of named areas,
	each with its own look, hazards and route choices (see "Track design" in
	CLAUDE.md). Rainbow Skyway is the one plain "vanilla" track. A race should
	take about 1.5-2 minutes, which is around 7000-8500 studs of road.
	To check a layout, run this in Studio's command bar (with the game stopped):
		print(require(game.ServerStorage.TrackCheck:Clone())("Magma"))

	POINTS: the main road is a smooth curve through these, in order. X/Z move
	it around the map, Y makes hills. Keep neighbouring points roughly 100-160
	apart. A tight corner needs closer points (~20-30 apart, evenly spaced, like
	Windy Ledge's 90-degree turn): a short gap right next to a long one makes
	the curve bulge, and a corner tighter than the road is wide folds the road
	over itself. Every track starts at (0, 0, -80) heading the same way, so its
	start line is next to the lobby. Everyone finishes at the end of it.

	BRANCHES: side routes that split off the main road and join it again
	(a risky shortcut, a secret cave...). A branch has its own POINTS, optional
	`width` and its own FEATURES (their `point` numbers count along the branch).
	`respawns = false` leaves out its respawn points: fall off it and you're put
	back where you last were on the main road (before the branch started).
	Its first and last points must sit inside a main-road Room, at about the
	same height, so there's space for the junction. The builder lays the
	branch's ends right onto the main road's surface, and walls open up by
	themselves where the roads meet.

	Every road has slow OFF-ROAD strips (grass, ash, snow, mud) between the
	road and the walls, except where there are no walls.

	COINS and ITEM BOXES are placed on every track automatically, clear of
	jumps and hazards (see PICKUPS and buildPickups in RaceServer).

	FEATURES are things placed on a road. Single spots use `point` (which POINT),
	`ahead` (studs further along the road from that point; negative = before it)
	and `lane` (sideways from the middle of the road; negative = left):
	  "Boost"     speed pad. Optional `time` (seconds of boost, if longer than usual)
	              and `width` (studs across, if wider than the usual 10)
	  "Ramp"      jump. Optional `angle` and `length`, plus `gap` = studs of
	              missing road after the lip. Too slow and you fall through!
	              `noReturn = true`: fall in and you're put back AFTER the gap
	              (not before the jump), and a low wall on the far edge stops
	              anyone driving back into the hole
	  "Banner"    arch over the road with `text` on it (name each area!)
	  "Sign"      signpost with `text`, just outside the wall on `side` ("left"/"right")
	  "Waterfall" a curtain of water right across the road (drive through it)
	  "Rock" / "Crystal" / "Snowman" / "Tree"   solid obstacle to steer around. `size`
	  "LavaPool"  touch it and you're back at your last checkpoint. `radius`
	  "Geyser"    vent (lava, or water in the jungle) that erupts every few
	              seconds and throws karts in the air. `radius`, `height`,
	              `period` (seconds per cycle), `active` (seconds it erupts),
	              `offset` (shifts its timing)
	  "FireBar"   spinning bar of fireballs that knocks karts aside.
	              `length`, `speed` (degrees per second, negative = other way)
	  "Snowball" / "Boulder"   rolls from side to side across the road.
	              `radius`, `period`, `offset`, optional `span` (how far it rolls)
	  "Frog"      hops across the road and back, crouching before each hop.
	              `period` (seconds there and back), `hops` (each way),
	              `height`, `offset`, optional `span`. `reverse = true` starts
	              it on the right: put one a few studs after a normal one, with
	              the same timing, and they pass each other in the middle.
	              Or `gap` (average seconds from one hop to the next) to hop at
	              random moments instead; give a pair the same `seed` to keep
	              them moving together
	  "Tongue"    a frog-head idol beside the road (`side`) whose tongue lashes
	              `reach` studs across it at random moments; its throat swells
	              and it croaks first. `period` (average seconds between lashes,
	              default 3), `active` (seconds a lash lasts). Keep `reach`
	              short of the far side so there's always a way past
	  "Respawn"   a respawn point right here. They're placed by themselves every
	              so often, but never near hazards or obstacles: use this where
	              that leaves a long stretch without one (a clearing in a forest)
	Sections of road use `from` and `to` points instead:
	  "Room"      the road opens out to `width`: an arena, plaza or cavern
	  "NoWalls"   no barriers (or off-road), so you can drive or get pushed off
	  "Ice"       slippery road. Optional `grip` (default ICE_GRIP)
	  "Rough"     the whole road is slow off-road (a grass or mud cut)
	  "Bridge"    wooden planks instead of road (pair it with NoWalls for a rope bridge)
	  "Tunnel"    a cave over the road
	  "Wind"      gusts that blow karts sideways. `push` (+ = to the right),
	              `period`, `active`, `offset`
	  "Flat"      no leaning into corners (keep a turn right before a hill from
	              twisting the road as it starts to climb)
	  "Skippable" main road that a drop or jump shortcut lets racers skip, so
	              no checkpoints go there (branches do this by themselves)
]]

-- Adds a thick forest to a list of FEATURES: `rows` rows of trees across the
-- road, `spacing` studs apart starting at `point`, with trees `gap` studs
-- apart across it (out to `reach` either side). Every other row is shifted
-- half a gap, so there's no straight line through: you have to weave.
-- `clearing` lists rows to leave out (counting from 0), for an open space.
local function forest(features, point: number, rows: number, spacing: number, gap: number, reach: number, clearing: { number }?)
	for row = 0, rows - 1 do
		if clearing and table.find(clearing, row) then
			continue
		end
		local k = 0
		for lane = -reach + (row % 2) * gap / 2, reach, gap do
			k += 1
			local wobble = ((row * 7 + k * 5) % 5 - 2) * 1.5 -- (so it isn't a perfect grid)
			table.insert(features, { kind = "Tree", point = point, ahead = row * spacing + wobble, lane = lane - wobble, size = 6 + (row + k) % 3 })
		end
	end
	return features
end

